socket (:socket s)) (assoc s :phase :closed)))))\n   226\t      (when (= :failed (invoke-close! @socket 1000 \"dao.stream/detached\"))\n   227\t        (emit-reason! state attachment :ws/error :ws/close-failure))\n   228\t      (outcome :dao.stream/ok))))\n   229\t\n   230\t\n   231\t(defn close-ended!\n   232\t  \"Transport-specific close for a serving composition whose source ended.\n   233\t\n   234\t   Generic `stream/close!` is a reattachable detachment.  This helper sends the\n   235\t   WebSocket ended signal (4000 / `dao.stream/ended`) so the peer can deposit\n   236\t   `:ws/ended`.  The host close callback still owns exactly-once terminal event\n   237\t   delivery, just as it does for generic close.\"\n   238\t  [handle]\n   239\t  (let [state (.-state ^WsHandle handle)\n   240\t        attachment (.-attachment ^WsHandle handle)\n   241\t        socket (volatile! nil)]\n   242\t    (swap! state\n   243\t           (fn [s]\n   244\t             (if (= :closed (:phase s))\n   245\t               s\n   246\t               (do (vreset! socket (:socket s)) (assoc s :phase :closed)))))\n   247\t    (when (= :failed (invoke-close! @socket ended-close-code \"dao.stream/ended\"))\n   248\t      (emit-reason! state attachment :ws/error :ws/close-failure))\n   249\t    (outcome :dao.stream/ok)))\n   250\t\n   251\t\n   252\t(defn- new-handle\n   253\t  [descriptor attachment target phase socket codec]\n   254\t  (let [state (atom {:phase phase :socket socket :deposit target\n   255\t                     :resolution? false :terminal? false})]\n   256\t    [(WsHandle. state descriptor attachment codec) state]))\n   257\t\n   258\t\n   259\t(defn- protocol-failure!\n   260\t  [state attachment]\n   261\t  (emit-reason! state attachment :ws/error :ws/decode-failure)\n   262\t  (swap! state assoc :phase :closed)\n   263\t  (invoke-close! (:socket @state) protocol-close-code \"dao.stream/protocol-error\"))\n   264\t\n   265\t\n   266\t(defn opened!\n   267\t  \"Adapter entry: the host socket itself is open -- for a client, its own\n   268\t   `connect!` seam calls this the moment the underlying connection\n   269\t   establishes, with no wire frame of ours to wait for; for a server,\n   270\t   `accept-slot!` sets `:open` directly and this stays a guarded no-op\n   271\t   for it.  There is no admission handshake any more: a served identity's\n   272\t   presence is the mirror's own answer, per identity, once attached.\"\n   273\t  [handle]\n   274\t  (let [state (.-state ^WsHandle handle)\n   275\t        attachment (.-attachment ^WsHandle handle)\n   276\t        emit? (volatile! false)]\n   277\t    (swap! state (fn [s]\n   278\t                   (if (and (= :connecting (:phase s)) (not (:resolution? s)))\n   279\t                     (do (vreset! emit? true) (assoc s :phase :open :resolution? true))\n   280\t                     s)))\n   281\t    (when @emit? (emit! state attachment :ws/opened))))\n   282\t\n   283\t\n   284\t(defn- deliver!\n   285\t  \"Decode one inbound frame payload with the handle's codec and dispatch its\n   286\t   envelope.  Wire validation only; application values are never judged.\n   287\t\n   288\t   There is no admission handshake any more, so a value frame can outrun\n   289\t   the server's own accept-slot! (the client's socket opens and its\n   290\t   append! gate is live from that moment, ahead of this endpoint's own\n   291\t   acceptor tick).  A frame arriving while still `:pending` is that race,\n   292\t   not a protocol violation: it is queued and replayed once `accept-slot!`\n   293\t   installs the real deposit target and opens the handle.\"\n   294\t  [handle payload]\n   295\t  (let [state (.-state ^WsHandle handle)\n   296\t        attachment (.-attachment ^WsHandle handle)\n   297\t        codec (.-codec ^WsHandle handle)]\n   298\t    (try\n   299\t      (let [frame ((:ws/decode codec) payload)]\n   300\t        (if-not (and (= :ws/value (:ws/frame frame))\n   301\t                     (contains? frame :ws/value)\n   302\t                     ((:ws/portable-value? codec) (:ws/value frame)))\n   303\t          (protocol-failure! state attachment)\n   304\t          (let [value (:ws/value frame)]\n   305\t            (case (:phase @state)\n   306\t              :open (emit! state attachment :ws/payload value)\n   307\t              :pending (swap! state update :pending-frames\n   308\t                              (fnil conj []) value)\n   309\t              (protocol-failure! state attachment)))))\n   310\t      (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n   311\t        (protocol-failure! state attachment)))))\n   312\t\n   313\t\n   314\t(defn receive!\n   315\t  \"Adapter entry for one text WebSocket message.  A text frame on a binary\n   316\t   profile is a protocol failure — the transport reads the frame's declared\n   317\t   kind, never its content.\"\n   318\t  [handle text]\n   319\t  (if (= :text (:ws/frame-kind (.-codec ^WsHandle handle)))\n   320\t    (deliver! handle text)\n   321\t    (protocol-failure! (.-state ^WsHandle handle)\n   322\t                       (.-attachment ^WsHandle handle))))\n   323\t\n   324\t\n   325\t(defn receive-binary!\n   326\t  \"Adapter entry for one binary WebSocket message (a host byte payload):\n   327\t   byte[] on the JVM, Uint8Array on ClojureScript, Uint8List on Dart.  A\n   328\t   binary frame on a text profile is a protocol failure.\"\n   329\t  [handle bytes]\n   330\t  (if (= :binary (:ws/frame-kind (.-codec ^WsHandle handle)))\n   331\t    (deliver! handle bytes)\n   332\t    (protocol-failure! (.-state ^WsHandle handle)\n   333\t                       (.-attachment ^WsHandle handle))))\n   334\t\n   335\t\n   336\t(defn closed!\n   337\t  \"Adapter entry for host close completion.  The terminal event is guarded so\n   338\t   close/error races cannot duplicate it.\"\n   339\t  [handle code _reason]\n   340\t  (let [state (.-state ^WsHandle handle)\n   341\t        attachme